## Best approach: adopt `System.IO.Abstractions`

This is a battle-tested NuGet package that wraps all `File.*`, `Directory.*`, `Path.*` statics behind injectable interfaces — no custom wrappers needed.

### 1. Install packages

```bash
dotnet add <YourProject> package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

### 2. Register in DI

```csharp
// Program.cs or Startup.cs
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes

```csharp
public class MyService(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public void Save(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public string Load(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public bool DirectoryExists(string path)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});
mockFs.AddDirectory("/data");

var service = new MyService(mockFs);

service.Save("/data/output.txt", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/data/output.txt"));
Assert.True(service.DirectoryExists("/data"));
```

---

**Why this over a custom wrapper?** `System.IO.Abstractions` covers the full `System.IO` surface, ships `MockFileSystem` for in-memory testing, and avoids you maintaining a growing custom interface as you use more file APIs.

Once you've installed the package and registered it in DI, use the `migrate-static-to-wrapper` skill (or ask me) to bulk-replace your existing `File.*`/`Directory.*` call sites with the injected `IFileSystem`.
